while relations between North and South  Korea remain tense a day after Pyongyang  threatened to restart military exercises  in the Demilitarized Zone tensions on  the Korean Peninsula hit a new low on  Tuesday when Pyongyang blew up its joint  liaison office with Seoul up until a  year ago the two had been making steps  toward a more peaceful coexistence  there's little sign of any of that now  it was hailed as the big moment of peace  and reconciliation the opening of the  north south korea joint liaison office  in September 2018 part of the two  countries efforts to reduce tensions on  the peninsula  now those hopes and the building lie in  ruins the two countries are technically  still at war that 50-year old conflict  ended in the truce not an actual peace  treaty after years of peace efforts a  breakthrough came with the South Korean  2018 Winter Olympics the two rivals even  announced the creation of a single  unified women's ice hockey team to enter  the games hopes kept growing with an  inter-korean summit a few months later  Chong yang in Seoul promised to pursue a  denuclearization of the peninsula  there were even talks of a permanent  peace deal then the moment many thought  would never happen a u.s. president  setting foot on North Korean soil Donald  Trump held face-to-face meetings with  the North's Kim jong-un  but the much-hyped talks didn't amount  to much last November the North again  increased its long-range missile tests  but win North Korean defectors based in  South Korea recently sent balloons with  propaganda leaflets across the border  Pyongyang threatened with consequences  even military action the comments  announced on North Korean TV were  attributed to Kim Jong Un's  influential sister kim yo-jong not the  leader himself she's a close ally who's  become one of the regime's most powerful  voices in recent years these latest  developments are not only a big setback  to ongoing peace efforts but also a  personal defeat for South Korea's  president moon jae-in whose  administration had been pushing for  further engagement with the north and  let's get more now we are joined by  Robert Kelley who is a political analyst  on inter-korean affairs and an associate  professor in political science at Pusan  National University he joins us now  welcome to the program tell us because  we have North Korea threatening to move  its military into the demilitarized  border areas how high is the risk of a  further escalation umm well them the  movement back wouldn't actually be too  big a deal the two Koreas signed in the  last couple years  a few agreements to sort of retrench a  little bit back from the demilitarized  on the Demilitarized Zone assist the  strip that runs right through the middle  of the country and there's no military  forces there well there are but not much  there the real issue is that the North  Koreans had sort of offered so to begin  to sort of pull back a little bit  they've got an enormous amount of force  very close to the South Korean capital  and that looks like it will be unwound  but it doesn't really threaten South  Korean security very much I think the  real issue in the in the next eight  months or so maybe six months to a year  is whether or not the North Koreans will  actually launch some kind of armed  provocation against South Korea they've  done this kind of stuff for decades um  that hasn't happened in several years I  think that's the real possible  escalatory step that people worry about  Pyongyang saying that the tensions are  over North Korean defectors sending  propaganda over the border that's  nothing new  so I mean why why is this happening now  yeah it's probably not because of the  leaf  the North Koreans have endured that for  they don't like it obviously and they've  taken actions against it in the past but  but the demolition of the building  probably wasn't really about that I  think it was really sort of a signal and  there's been a buildup of North Korean  rhetoric in the last few months and I  think this is sort of a culmination of  that rhetoric there's been this sort of  effort to sort of get moon Jane the  president of South Korea to act  independently of the Americans in the  international community and strike a  deal on North Korea the North Koreans  really want sanctions relief but moon  Jane really can't do that because one  legally he's bound by the rules at the  UN and then politically he's bound by  this for constraints of the American  alliance the Alliance is very popular in  South Korea and so even if moon would  like to go off and do something  independently he really can't because he  would face massive blowback here and so  this is kind of left him stuck and the  North Koreans have been pushing and kind  of bullying him and this was the latest  step in that that's what this is really  about they're trying to get a deal out  of the South okay so they're trying to  get a deal let's talk about the face  also that has become very prominent in  all of this the face of last week's  escalation it's been not North Korean  leader Kim jong-un as we might expect  but rather his sister why is that yeah  there's so there's a lot of speculation  about this right sort of the the rise of  the sister in the background and the  most obvious reason probably is because  North Korea is a bloodline monarchy and  she is the most obvious person to take  over in that we don't know if Kim  jong-un has children there's sort of  rumor people think he does but they  would be too young right and so we kind  of assumed that the sister would have to  sort of take over there's a sort of an  interesting debate there about whether  or not the North Korean elites could  sort of accept the female ascension that  sort of another issue I oh that's that  that's one thing right because she's  close to Kim  she has sort of taken over stuff just  because of the bloodline relationship  and then also I think to a certain  extent Kim jong-un wants to avoid  personally putting his hands on this  mess because if it does things do turn  around if things do get better you can  say oh you know that was my sister right  I'm still interested in peace so it's  kind of like a good cop bad cop okay and  I think so he has some plausible  deniability there Robert Kelley in Busan  putting all these tensions on the Korean  Peninsula into perspective for us so we  thank you very much for sharing your  expertise today thank you for having me  for let's bring in correspondent Frank  Smith in Seoul g'day to you Frank can  you take us through this latest  escalation how did we get to this point  well we need to go back to 2018 when we  had a couple of summits several summits  some between North Korean leader Kim  jong-un and US President Donald Trump  but for our purposes today the two  summits or three summits between South  Korean president moon jae-in and North  Korean leader Kim jong-un and they drew  up the so-called pan moon jom  Declaration and part of that involved a  drawdown of tension along the border an  elimination of propaganda activities  that had taken place you know between  the two Koreas including things like  loudspeakers set up along the border  blasting anti-north Korea messages  toward the north from the south and the  dismantling of guard posts on both sides  of the heavily militarized DMZ one of  the activities that had continued since  that time is the flying of leaflets  across the border by balloon by South  Korean NGOs nongovernmental  organizations led by North Korean  defectors one of those groups is led by  bak Jung oh and he sends rice and  medicine in some [ __ ]  sometimes lately anti coronavirus masks  in plastic bottles by the ocean and I  had a chance to talk to him earlier this  week North Korean defector Park jeong-ho  has been sending rice and medicine to  North Korea in plastic bottles using  ocean currents for four years now  following Pyongyang's recent complaints  South Korean officials have vowed to  stop such activities but Pok is  undeterred I don't know why the  government is trying to stop this now  they should say that there is nothing  they can do because this is a  non-governmental group activity I think  it would be wrong to suppress such  organizations because of what was said  by North Korea's Kim Yu  young I'm confused whether I live here  in Seoul or in Pyongyang okay Lee Jung  goes brother Park sang Hawk has been  sending leaflets critical of the Kim  regime by balloon over the border for  more than a decade  the two brothers fled North Korea some  20 years ago in call for South Korea to  push back against Pyongyang critics  though argued that actions like theirs  only provoke North Korea activists like  those gathered here today outside the  blue house South Korea's presidential  compound show the stark division in  public opinion over how to deal with  North Korea some advocate a hard-line  approach while others like most of those  gathered here today favor renewed  efforts at engagement and peacemaking  this civic group is calling for  president moon jae-in to implement the  deals he made in two landmark summits  with North Korean leader Kim jong-un in  2018 which includes suspending  cross-border propaganda no matter what  kind of non-governmental organization is  involved  the South Korean government can stop  them and the South Korean government  should stop them from sending fliers to  North Korea and should implement the pan  moon drum declaration after North Korea  Tuesday blew up the joint liaison office  in Kaesong just inside North Korea South  Korea offered to send an envoy a move  which was rejected by the north defector  Park jeong-ho is against any further  attempts at appeasement and wants South  Korea to stand up to North Korea if we  kneel North Korea will continue to move  in the same direction if we stay strong  our fifty million people can be free  from threats we need to have the  confidence of a sovereign state because  they are a card in the world  as the tension simmers and despite  warnings police will interfere Park  still plans to monitor the tide and send  bottles of rice to North Korea  Frank Pyongyang's blaming the tensions  on North Korean defectors like Park  jeong-ho who sent propaganda across the  border but this isn't anything new so so  why the harsh response from the north  well we're hearing a lot of different  analysis here in South Korea it's hard  to really divine North Korea's reasoning  behind some of its policies especially  in this case if we look at a couple of  things first of all kovat 19 has gripped  by many reports North Korea and so the  regime might be trying to distract  people from that but a lot of analysts  are saying this is messaging to the  United States over the ongoing United  Nations Security Council sanctions  against North Korea that prevent North  Korea from engaging the international  community especially in terms of  commerce so that's the the primary  takeaway it's not necessarily just about  these leaflets but it's about that that  greater issue of North Korea's isolation  now in front of the cameras the face of  the latest escalation has not been North  Korean leader Kim jong-un but instead  his sister kim yo-jong why is she  playing such a prominent role right now  well I think if we go back a month or  two ago when the reports came out in  North Korea that Kim jong-un was gravely  ill that later proved to be you know  somewhat false we were asking ourselves  who would be the next leader of North  Korea at that time guy interviewed isang  hyung of the Sejong Institute just  outside of Seoul and he said that if we  hear about kim yo-jong achievements in  her policy if she's more present in  North Korean propaganda then that means  she is more than likely the presumptive  heir to the North Korean regime to the  Kim dynasty and that's certain  what we've seen in this recent cycle of  escalated tension on the Korean  Peninsula Frank Smith's thanks much for  that from Seoul

